  <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy mt-2 mb-4">What makes this segment different</h2>
  <p>
    A party supplies distributor sells a $6 toy to a retailer for $4 and buys it for $2. There is no room in that chain
    for freight surprises, so the number that matters is not the FOB price — it is the landed cost per carton, and that
    is decided by how efficiently the carton fills a container.
  </p>
  <p>
    Party items are also unusually seasonal and unusually fragile in planning terms. A bubble machine ordered for a May
    promotion that arrives in June is worth less than the freight it travelled on. That is why the practical question for
    this segment is never "what is your best price" but "what is the latest ship date that still works", asked before the
    price is discussed.
  </p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy mt-10 mb-4">The three levers on landed cost</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Carton efficiency.</strong> A light, bulky bubble machine fills volume rather than weight, so freight per
      piece is set almost entirely by carton dimensions. Two suppliers quoting the same FOB price can be 15% apart on
      landed cost because one uses a carton 3 cm taller.</li>
    <li><strong>Quantity to the container.</strong> Crossing from a 60%-full container to a 90%-full one does more for
      your cost per piece than most published price tiers. Order to the container, not to the price break.</li>
    <li><strong>Consumable attach rate.</strong> If you also supply the bubble solution, the machine becomes a
      repeat-purchase item rather than a one-off. That changes the whole economics of the account.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy mt-10 mb-4">What to verify before the first pallet</h2>
  <p>
    Ask for the carton compression grade, the maximum stack height and the drop-test result. A party range that arrives
    with 8% crushed cartons is not a pricing problem, it is a packaging problem, and it is far cheaper to fix at the
    factory than to argue about with a retailer who has already refused the delivery.
  </p>
  <p>
    Also confirm what happens with the bubble liquid in transit. It is a liquid, some carriers restrict it, and a leaking
    bottle inside a carton damages the artwork of every unit around it. We ship with the bottle sealed separately inside
    the box for exactly that reason.
  </p>

